From: meta-exein <thomas@exein.io>
Subject: [PATCH] eBPF: satisfy the Linux 6.18 verifier in buffer_append_user_memory

process-monitor fails to start on Linux 6.18: the raw_tracepoint
sched_process_exec program is rejected at load time with

    ; int r = bpf_core_read_user(&((char *)buffer->buffer)[pos], len, source);
      @ buffer.bpf.h:121
    R2 min value is negative, either use unsigned or 'var &= const'

The caller in process-monitor computes the length as a u64

    u64 len = end > start ? end - start : 0;

from mm->arg_start / mm->arg_end, and buffer_append_user_memory is
__always_inline, so the verifier reasons about that 64-bit value rather
than the u32 parameter. The existing `len >= HALF_BUFFER_MASK` guard
compiles to a 32-bit compare, which bounds only the 32-bit range; the
64-bit smin stays negative, and a probe-read helper's size argument is
checked against the 64-bit bounds.

Masking alone does not help -- clang folds `len &= HALF_BUFFER_MASK` away,
having already proven the 32-bit range from the branch. barrier_var()
forces the value to be materialised first so the mask survives and
establishes a fresh [0, HALF_BUFFER_MASK] bound the verifier accepts. It
is a no-op at runtime: the guard above has already rejected anything
>= HALF_BUFFER_MASK (2^13-1).

Without this, process-monitor never starts, nothing populates the process
tracker, every event carries an unset header.image, and every rule keyed on
the calling binary silently stops matching -- while the daemon otherwise
appears healthy.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: meta-exein <thomas@exein.io>
diff --git a/crates/bpf-builder/include/buffer.bpf.h b/crates/bpf-builder/include/buffer.bpf.h
index 8cee569..8c8f1a8 100644
--- a/crates/bpf-builder/include/buffer.bpf.h
+++ b/crates/bpf-builder/include/buffer.bpf.h
@@ -118,6 +118,23 @@ static __always_inline int buffer_append_user_memory(struct buffer *buffer,
     return -1;
   }
 
+  // Linux 6.18 rejects the read below with "R2 min value is negative".
+  //
+  // The sole caller computes its length as a u64 (arg_end - arg_start read
+  // from mm) and this function is __always_inline, so the verifier reasons
+  // about that 64-bit value. The bound check above compiles to a 32-bit
+  // compare, which constrains only the 32-bit range and leaves the 64-bit
+  // smin negative -- and the size argument of a probe-read helper is checked
+  // against the 64-bit bounds.
+  //
+  // Masking alone is not enough: clang folds it away, having already proven
+  // the 32-bit range from the branch. barrier_var() forces the value to be
+  // materialised first, so the mask survives and gives the verifier a fresh
+  // [0, HALF_BUFFER_MASK] bound in both domains. No-op at runtime for every
+  // value that reaches here.
+  barrier_var(len);
+  len &= HALF_BUFFER_MASK;
+
   int r = bpf_core_read_user(&((char *)buffer->buffer)[pos], len, source);
   if (r < 0) {
     LOG_ERROR("reading failure: %d", r);
